The word spoken to them is "the word", i.e. the word of punishment (kalimat al-'adhab). Its apparent meaning is shown by His saying: ﴿And had We willed, We would have given every soul its guidance, but the word from Me has come into effect: "I will surely fill Hell with jinn and mankind all together."﴾ [al-Sajdah: 13], and other similar verses.

As for His saying in this noble verse, ﴿and they will not be able to speak﴾, its apparent meaning is that the disbelievers will not speak on the Day of Resurrection, as is understood from His saying: ﴿This is a Day when they will not speak, nor will they be permitted to offer an excuse﴾ [al-Mursalat: 35].

Against this, there is:

• His saying: ﴿And We will gather them on the Day of Resurrection upon their faces, blind, mute and deaf﴾ [al-Isra': 97], together with other verses of the Book of Allah which clearly show that they will speak on the Day of Resurrection and will offer excuses;

• such as His saying about them: ﴿By Allah, our Lord, we were not polytheists﴾ [al-An'am: 23];

• and His saying about them: ﴿And they will offer submission: "We did not do any evil"﴾ [al-Nahl: 28];

• and His saying: ﴿If only you could see when the criminals will hang their heads before their Lord: "Our Lord, we have now seen and heard, so send us back, and we will do righteous deeds"﴾ [al-Sajdah: 12];

• and His saying about them: ﴿Our Lord, our wretchedness overcame us, and we were a misguided people. Our Lord, bring us out of it, and if we return, then we will be wrongdoers﴾ [al-Mu'minun: 106–107];

• and His saying: ﴿And they will call out: "O Malik!"﴾ [al-Zukhruf: 77] (p. 144);

and other verses indicating their speech on the Day of Resurrection.

We have already explained the answer to this in our book "Daf' Iham al-Idtirab 'an Ayat al-Kitab" (Removing the Appearance of Contradiction from the Verses of the Book), in the commentary on Surah al-Mursalat, concerning His saying: ﴿This is a Day when they will not speak﴾ [al-Mursalat: 35], and the verses we mentioned. We stated there that among the answers to this is that the Resurrection has several stations (mawatin): in some of them they speak, and in some of them they do not speak. So affirming speech for them and negating it from them are both applied to a state and a time different from the other's state and time. Another answer is that their affirmed speech is specific to what is of no benefit to them, while the negated speech is specific to what would benefit them, and there are other answers. We have mentioned some of the answers to this in our commentaries on "al-Furqan", "Ta-Ha", and "al-Isra'".
